[Music]  today is primary election day in new  york city  and voters are making history when they  fill out their ballots the top contest  on  is the democratic party's race for mayor  the winner will become the overwhelming  favorite to be elected in november  this primary will also be a big test for  a system called  ranked choice voting voters are asked to  pick their top  five choices instead of choosing just  one candidate  ed o'keefe is covering the primary for  us ed good morning good to see you in  person mr good morning everyone ranked  choice has been used in other cities  but if all goes well here in this  primary race in new york today it could  lead other cities and states to adopt  the method  rank choice kicks in if one candidate  doesn't get at least 50 percent of the  vote  and with 13 democratic candidates on the  primary ballot today  that's likely to happen stay focused  don't get distracted grind and vote this  year in new york  big apple voters are making big choices  in the race to succeed mayor bill de  blasio  are you guys ready to get out the vote  i am all about getting people out to  vote  top issues of concern include rebuilding  from the pandemic and affordable housing  but especially crime and policing where  people don't feel safe on our streets  and on our subways  murders in new york are up nearly 14  percent year over year  shootings up 64 percent yes i do it's  caused a rift between candidates like  brooklyn borough president and former  police captain eric adams  who supports more traditional policing  tactics this is more than just  an increased number in police officers  but really  how are we deploying them whole city  and civil rights attorney maya wiley  who's a leading liberal in the race and  wants to redistribute a billion dollars  out of the nypd's budget i call it being  smart and growing public safety and  right size in the police department  this year for the first time new yorkers  are ranking their choices for mayor  ranked choice voting has been used for  years in san francisco and minneapolis  and across the state of maine  but let's face it new york is new york  and with apologies to frank sinatra if  it can make it there  the hope is it would make it anywhere if  rachel's voting can make it in new york  city  i think it will make it anywhere rob  richie is ceo of fair vote a  non-partisan group that pushes for  election reforms  like ranked choice voting right now the  current system really isn't a level  playing field so  conventional wisdom kind of runs the day  right um  and so rancher's voting allows voters to  sort out the candidates  and give them a fair shot and you can  vote for your favorite without  worrying whether that candidate has you  know passes the viability test  here's how it works voters can rank up  to five candidates  if one receives more than 50 percent of  first-choice votes  they're the winner if not the candidate  with the fewest votes is eliminated  and those votes go to the politician  people rank next on the ballot  a process that repeats until it's down  to two candidates  and one has the most votes the change in  voting has led to a change in strategy  two top contenders former presidential  candidate andrew yang and former  sanitation commissioner catherine garcia  have teamed up on the trail if you do  support me please also support catherine  garcia on your ballot  garcia doesn't explicitly endorse yang  but says it's about promoting the new  voting method  clear i'm not co-endorsing we are  campaigning together we are promoting  rank choice voting  some voters say the new system compelled  them to pay closer attention  i didn't want to just choose anyone to  fill those spaces but i really did the  research and wanted to make sure i was  making an educated decision  and while many like it for me it was a  piece of cake man  others say forget about it i don't know  i think it's confusing  all right new yorkers pack your patience  because it could be weeks  until we know a winner due to the  state's absentee ballot rules the  campaigns we spoke to say  there's so much uncertainty about who  might win because of the crowded field  and this new way of voting this is going  to be a really good question it's not  just who you put on your ballot but who  you leave off  that's part of the campaign too and  those and there are those charges that  you know  people argue it might disenfranchise  certain that's because  hot button debate just in the last few  days of you know is this potentially  causing  especially candidates of color  potentially to suffer  the others turn around and say no  because now everyone  in essence if you put your top five down  it's very likely that one of your five  potentially is the winner but everybody  has to vote for everybody  right well yeah that's one thing you got  to fill out all five slots  because otherwise remember that uh  runoff election we were waiting for in  georgia it took several weeks the  proponents of this system say this is  your runoff yeah and so we can get this  done in several days or weeks instead of  months that's the idea  all right ed thanks so much

